# No live row and no dispatched lane, deliberately: the parks this scenario is about belong to no
# lane at all, and an in-flight session would arm caffeinate once per tick. The shim appends to
# calls.log detached and on its own time, so those arms race the next listing call and the
# expectation flakes on ordering alone (tests/run.sh says so; D-172 is what that costs).
echo "== limitation 33's second half. A handover Baton could not parse, and whose session it could"
echo "== not place in any lane, leaves a park with no project: no ruling target, no re-read receipt,"
echo "== and no per-project pass that will ever visit it. Four such parks, and only one of them may"
echo "== close on this tick:"
echo "==   M05 - its session, started by hand, went on to write an artifact Baton could read and"
echo "==         consume for the same milestone. Nothing was typed at Baton; the park closes."
echo "==   M04 - its file is still sitting in rejected/ and nothing has been corrected. Stands."
echo "==   M06 - its session had a handover consumed since, but for M01, a different milestone. The"
echo "==         M06 file is untouched, so nothing about M06 has been corrected. Stands."
echo "==   M07 - its session's consumed handover is EARLIER in the log, and only its at string"
echo "==         sorts later, because that event carries a -05:00 offset and the park carries +01:00."
echo "==         Log order is what decides (CONTRACT clause 3(c)), so it stands. Reading the at as"
echo "==         text instead would close it, which is what this row exists to catch."
"$BATON" tick || echo "status $?"
"$BATON" status
echo
echo "== the person deals with M04's file. They type nothing at Baton, because there is nothing they"
echo "== could have typed: the park names no lane to answer. The next tick reads the condition itself."
rm -f "$BATON_HOME/rejected/M04-orphan-session-id.json"
"$BATON" tick || echo "status $?"
"$BATON" status
